Z||000033||fyerstarter||06-03-2001||09:58 PM||||About a year and a half and I'm going on my second GT now. First one is an 87 GT manual with some performance mods and the new one is an 86 GT auto (blah) stock with 70k miles and rebuilt motor. One thing I have learned in my short time with Fieros is that either they are the least reliable cars on the road, or I have the worst luck on the road. I am actually afraid to turn up my stereo while driving because I wan't to listen to the engine and intercept potential problems. Driving a Fiero is like a self induced paranoia, "what is going to break and leave me stranded next?". <P>But after all this I still went and bought another one and I still love them more than any other car I've owned. I am convinced that Fieros are satan in disguise :\||||reg||1
Z||000034||Pontiaddict||06-03-2001||11:05 PM||||I got my 84 as a first car. Had it for a year and sold it to get a GT. 3 years later, I now have a 86 GT that I bought last June.<p>[This message has been edited by Pontiaddict (edited 06-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000035||Rodrv6||06-03-2001||11:44 PM||||I bought mine new in February, 1984.  Still have it with around 330,000+ miles on it and 1 total rebuild (down to the bare spaceframe).  I added an 88 GT to the garage in January of this year.  Now the 84 doesn't get driven as much---V-6's are a lot of fun <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||7
Z||000036||PAUL88GT||06-03-2001||11:51 PM||||86 Coupe: bought at auction in 1997, sold in 1998.  This one got me hooked.<P>88GT: all options except t-tops, bought in 1998, rarely driven. Great condition, but high mileage (136k miles). I plan to keep this one for a while<P>86SE:  Bought in 2/01.  This car is fully loaded, but it was not well maintained.  I am in the process of a major restoration. I plan to drive this one a few days a week to keep the miles off my 88GT. I may trade this car for another Fiero after driving it for a while. ||||reg||1
Z||000037||sqoach||06-04-2001||12:21 AM||||Got mine in March of 2000. That's when I was looking for my first car. I was always checking out autotrader for something, but nothing I found was in my price range that I liked. So I asked my parents if they knew of any sporty type cars that I might like. They mentioned some, but when they got to describing the Fiero, I had to look for one. And I don't know why, either. Just sounded cool and I had never heard of one. I found my 86 SC on autotrader, and without even considering looking anymore, and went down to OKC. I really wanted an 85 GT or 86 SE V6, but bought it anyway. It's been fun, but I've worked on it more than I've driven it, so it's kind of bummed me out a bit.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> But when I finally get it fixed, I'll probably sell it. Then I can get my 86 SE V6 and hopefully not have the same problems  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach2.JPG">   <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||1
Z||000038||fierojunky||06-04-2001||11:33 AM||||Got Mine when i turned 16 so 6 years now and many more to come. <P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierojunkysig.jpg">    <BR>All show and no go is like a Wonder Bra. Looks really good untill you open it up||||reg||6
